{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790265033,"nonce":"EOpvK5hROvEts/TkMHLiY+hr/2Y=","title":"Array\u003cDynamic\u003e literal of uniform reference elements stores them raw; field reads return null","message":"var s:Array\u003cDynamic\u003e = [\"x\", \"y\"];  trace(s[1].length);   // Haxe 1, rayzor null\n    var o:Array\u003cDynamic\u003e = [{v: 3}];     trace(o[0].v);         // 3 vs null\n    var n:Array\u003cDynamic\u003e = [[1, 2]];     trace(n[0].length);    // 2 vs null\n    f([[\"a\"]]) with f(a:Array\u003cDynamic\u003e) { switch a { case [[\"a\"]]: ... } }  // never matched (Issue3377)\n\nThe literal's type is its first element's, so it is Array\u003cString\u003e etc.; only heterogeneous register types were boxed. Readers treat an Array\u003cDynamic\u003e element as a box.\n\nFix: a literal lowered against Array\u003cDynamic\u003e (declared var, or argument for a non-extern Array\u003cDynamic\u003e parameter) boxes its reference elements via maybe_box_value; nested literals under it are Array\u003cDynamic\u003e too. Scalars stay raw: Type.createInstance's ctor wrappers and erased-generic readers (GenericCallTypeArgs fixture) take them raw, and a raw class instance or string pointer cannot be told from a box at runtime (tag_is_known accepts any header word \u003e 100), so extern callees (runtime-implemented, e.g. Type.createInstance) keep raw arrays.","files":null}]}